#dnl -*- mode: web; indent-tabs-mode: t -*-
#define PAGE_TITLE %{PAGE_DOCS}
#include ../inc/header.in
<h1>My First skonfig Type</h1>

<p>
	Writing a skonfig type is easy, unlike in some other configuration
	management solutions.
</p>

<h3>How does a type work?</h3>
<p>
	When a type is instantiated (i.e. an object is created), the following steps
	are executed by the skonfig runtime:
	<ol>
		<li><b>type explorers</b> are executed on the target</li>
		<li>the <b><code>manifest</code></b> is executed (manifests can create other objects to use existing types)</li>
		<li>the <b><code>gencode-local</code></b> script is executed to deduce the
			shell code required to be executed on the config host to bring the
			target into the desired state)</li>
		<li>the <b><code>gencode-remote</code></b> script is executed to deduce the
			shell code required to be executed on the target to bring the
			target into the desired state)</li>
		<li>once all <code>require</code>s are fulfilled, the code generated by
			the <code>gencode-*</code> scripts is executed
			(<code>code-local</code> first, then <code>code-remote</code>)</li>
	</ol>
</p>

<h3>Implementing a simple type to configure <code>/etc/motd</code></h3>
<p>
	In this section we are going to implement a simplified version of the
	<b>cdist-type__motd</b>(7) to demonstrate how types work in principle.
</p>
<p>
	While we could re-use <b>cdist-type__file</b>(7) from the base set to manage
	files and their contents (and you usually would), we will implemenent the
	type without re-using existing code to show the principle of idempotency.
</p>

<p>
	Before we start implementing we have to think about the design of our new
	type; how do we want to be able to use it?
</p>

<p>
	For our <code>__simple_motd</code> type we will implement the following
	interface:

	<code><pre>
usage: __simple_motd --message MESSAGE

options:
  --message MESSAGE
	</pre></code>
</p>

<p>
	First, we create the base layout for the new type. We could do this manually
	using <b>mkdir</b>(1), but skonfig has a helper script
	<code>skonfig-new-type</code> which automates the process for us:
</p>

<p>
	<code><pre>
$ skonfig-new-type
usage: skonfig-new-type TYPE-NAME AUTHOR-NAME AUTHOR-EMAIL [TYPE-BASE-PATH]
	TYPE-NAME       Name of the type.
	AUTHOR-NAME     Type author's full name.
	AUTHOR-EMAIL    Type author's email.
	TYPE-BASE-PATH  Path to the base directory of the type. If not set it defaults
	                to '$PWD/type'.

$ skonfig-new-type __simple_motd 'You' 'you@example.com' ~/.skonfig/type
	</pre></code>
</p>

<p>
	Now, we declare our <code>--message</code> parameter:

	<code><pre>
$ cd ~/.skonfig/type/__simple_motd
$ mkdir parameter
$ mkdir parameter/default
$ echo message >>parameter/required
$ echo 'Today is a good day to have a good day.' >parameter/default/message
	</pre></code>
</p>

<p>
	As the next step we have to think about which information we need to be
	able to check the state of the target and bring it into the desired state.
</p>
<p>
	For our <code>__simple_motd</code> type we need the <b>stat</b>(3)
	information to manage ownership and permissions and the current contents of
	the file to compare it with the message given by the user of the type.
</p>
<p>We create the two explorers as follows:</p>
<p>
	<code>~/.skonfig/type/__simple_motd/explorer/stat</code>:
	<code><pre>#!/bin/sh -e

ls -ldn /etc/motd 2>/dev/null | {
	read -r _mode _ _owner _group _ || exit 0

	# mode:owner(uid):group(gid)
	printf '%s:%s:%s\\n' "${_mode}" "${_owner}" "${_group}"
}
	</pre></code>
</p>
<p>
	<code>~/.skonfig/type/__simple_motd/explorer/message</code>:
	<code><pre>#!/bin/sh -e

if test -r /etc/motd
then
	cat /etc/motd
fi
	</pre></code>
</p>

<p>
	Now, with this information we can continue to implement the logic which
	configures the target system.
	For this simple type we don't need a <code>manifest</code> because we don't
	re-use any existing types nor a <code>gencode-local</code> (it is rarely
	needed, usually only when files or other information has to be copied over
	to the target first).
</p>

<p>
	<code>~/.skonfig/type/__simple_motd/explorer/message</code>:
	<code><pre>#!/bin/sh -e

# if               current /etc/motd                 the --message given
if ! cmp -s "${__object:?}/explorer/message" "${__object:?}/parameter/message"
then
	printf 'cat "${__object:?}/parameter/message" >/etc/motd\\n'
fi

IFS=: read -r mode_is uid_is gid_is &lt;"${__object:?}/explorer/stat" || :

if test "${uid_is}:${gid_is}" != '0:0'
then
	printf 'chown 0:0 /etc/motd\\n'
fi
if test "${mode_is#?}" != 'rw-r--r--'
then
	printf 'chmod 0644 /etc/motd\\n'
fi
	</pre></code>
</p>
#include ../inc/footer.in
